    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d540">

                <pb facs="0754" n="674" sameAs="frus:frus1936v01/pg674"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">641.0031/66: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in the United Kingdom</hi> (<hi
                        rendition="#italic">Bingham</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary
                        of State </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">London</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1936-07-09T18:00:00+01:00">July 9, 1936—6
                            p.m.</date>
                        <lb/> [Received July 9—1:05 p.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>341. Until Eden, who is away for a week, returns I cannot appropriately take up
                    your 234, July 3, 4 p.m., with any other Cabinet Minister. I shall, however, act
                    on it at the earliest practicable <pb facs="0755" n="675" xml:id="pg675"/>
                    moment. Before doing so I venture to suggest for your consideration that the
                    second sentence of your memorandum be changed to read as follows: “Should the
                    issuance of this statement be long deferred it would be a disappointment of
                    corresponding magnitude”.<anchor n="38" xml:id="d540-f1"/> Although Runciman’s
                    and Chamberlain’s statements contained in the Embassy’s 323, June 25, 5 p.m.,
                    and 326, June 26, 6 p.m., were undoubtedly authoritative, nevertheless no
                    official answer has been made by Eden to the memorandum contained in your 214,
                    June 18, 6 p.m., and it would also seem to be desirable tactics not to proceed
                    on the assumption that a hard and fast decision had been reached.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Bingham</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d540-f1" n="38" xml:id="d540-f1-content">
                <p>By telegram No. 246, July 10, 1 p.m., the Ambassador was informed of the
                    Secretary’s approval of the suggested change.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
